TORCS - The Open Racing Car Simulator, for RISC OS
===================================================

TORCS 1.3.7 (Debian's free-software source), ported to RISC OS by Andrew
Youll (riscos-torcs). This is a TEST BUILD.

Requirements
------------
- A Raspberry Pi 4 (or another fast ARMv7 machine) with RISC OS 5.
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (from PackMan).
- For sound: SharedSound (in !System), plus StreamManager and
  SharedSoundBuffer (ssb.zip from orac.co.uk). Without them TORCS is
  silent.
- About 64 MB of free memory.

Everything is drawn by software OpenGL (riscos-mesa) on the CPU, so the
fewer pixels it draws, the faster it runs. TORCS opens in a desktop
window 540 pixels high, in the screen's shape (960x540 on a 16:9
screen). It can also run full screen (see Options): the picture is then
drawn at the same reduced size and stretched over the screen, by the
Pi's display hardware where the VideoOverlay module is loaded (!Run
loads it if it's in !System). Other programs keep running underneath.

Playing
-------
Double-click !TORCS. In the menus, click an item or use the cursor keys
and Return; Escape goes back.

Race > Quick Race > New Race starts a race. This build starts with you
alone on the track (no computer drivers), so you can see how fast it
runs. Add computer drivers in Race > Quick Race > Configure Race.

Driving (keyboard, the default):
  Cursor Up / Down      throttle / brake
  Cursor Left / Right   steer
  (gears are automatic; gear keys can be set in Configure Players)
  F2 - F11              camera views
  P                     pause
  Escape                the race menu (abandon, restart...)

Configure Players sets your name, car and controls; a USB joystick
works through USBJoystick (0.23 or later) or any Joystick module.

Options
-------
- Options > Display: the drawing size and Full-screen or Window mode.
  In a window it's the window's size; in full screen it's what's drawn
  and then stretched over the screen. Pick one in your screen's shape. Smaller is faster, bigger is sharper; below 480
  high the writing gets blocky.
    4:3    480x360  640x480  720x540
    16:10  576x360  768x480  864x540  1152x720
    16:9   640x360  856x480  960x540  1280x720
  TORCS saves the setting and quits; start it again to use it.
- Options > Graphic Configuration:
  - Scene resolution (%): the size the 3D scene (track, cars) is drawn at,
    as a percentage of the screen size, stretched to fill it. The writing
    and the race display are always drawn at the full size. Default 50:
    a quarter of the pixels. 100 is sharpest and slowest; 33-75 trade
    sharpness for speed.
  - Visibility, smoke, skid marks, wheels, and the sky background (the
    panorama around the track; off by default: it costs a fifth of the
    drawing time).
- The !Run file has settings: a log file (TORCS$Log, on in test builds:
  <Wimp$ScrapDir>.TORCSLog), and -M for multitexturing (slower).
- Your settings and race results are in <Choices$Write>.TORCS. Delete
  that directory to go back to the defaults.

Problems
--------
If TORCS stops with an error, the log (<Wimp$ScrapDir>.TORCSLog) has
the RISC OS error and a backtrace: please send it.

